+*   **Summation:** Some modes simply sum interpolated scalar values, as in X-ray rendering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Path Tracing:** Others trace randomized photon paths to solve the multi-dimensional rendering equation, accounting for scattering and global illumination [S3, unofficial][S10].
 *   **Layered Compositing:** Some render distinct material styles into separate layers and composite them post-hoc [S2].
-*   **Occlusion-Aware Shading:** Others integrate in-scattered radiance approximations using cone-shaped phase functions during slice traversal [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [TVAM Integrator](6814-tvam-integrator.md)
-The TVAM Integrator is a base class for volumetric integrators in Tomographic Volumetric Additive Manufacturing (TVAM) that handles projector ray sampling, time sampling, and regular versus stochastic sampling modes. It solves the problem of generating correct ray samples for the projector emitter in a TVAM simulation, distinguishing between deterministic pixel-center sampling for regular modes and stochastic sampling for Monte Carlo integration. It sets itself apart from sibling integrators like the [Radon Integrator](7470-radon-integrator.md) by explicitly managing the temporal dimension of the DMD pattern sequence and the specific geometry of the projector emitter, rather than just performing a static projection.
+A base class for volumetric additive manufacturing integrators that handles projector ray sampling, time sampling, and regular versus stochastic sampling modes. It sets itself apart from sibling integrators by focusing on the temporal and geometric sampling of projector emissions rather than material compositing. Choose this method when simulating volumetric additive manufacturing processes requiring precise projector emission sampling.
 
 ### [Cinematic Rendering](9473-cinematic-rendering.md)
-Cinematic Rendering (CR) is a physically based, real-time volume rendering technique for medical image data (CT/MRI) that solves the multi-dimensional, non-continuous rendering equation by integrating light scattered from all possible directions along a ray, generating photo-realistic depictions using random sampling. It differs from traditional Direct Volume Ray Casting [19-direct-volume-ray-casting.md] by tracing hundreds or thousands of photon paths per pixel (path tracing) rather than a single straight ray, thereby accounting for complex light-matter interactions such as scattering, absorption, multiple bounces, and shadowing. Unlike conventional VR which relies on local gradient shading and synthetic light sources, CR uses High Dynamic Range (HDR) light maps and Monte Carlo integration to simulate global illumination effects like soft shadows, ambient occlusion, and subsurface scattering.
-
-### [Directional Occlusion Shading](10970-directional-occlusion-shading.md)
-Directional Occlusion Shading (DOS) is an image-order shading technique for interactive direct volume rendering that approximates in-scattered radiance using a cone-shaped phase function to model directional occlusion. It provides perceptual cues similar to ambient occlusion for solid and transparent surface-like features without precomputation. Unlike half-angle slicing, DOS does not adapt the slicing axis to the light position; instead, it assumes a single light source at the camera position and uses a backward-peaked phase function to integrate soft shadowing effects during front-to-back slice traversal.
+A physically based, real-time volume rendering technique for medical image data (CT/MRI) that solves the multi-dimensional, non-continuous rendering equation by integrating light scattered from all possible directions along a ray. It sets itself apart by tracing hundreds or thousands of photon paths per pixel to simulate complex light-matter interactions, including multiple scattering and global illumination effects like soft shadows. Choose this method when photo-realistic medical visualization with accurate lighting is required.
 
 ### [Compositional Rendering](11182-compositional-rendering.md)
-Compositional Rendering is a method for integrating newly generated 3D objects into an existing Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) scene by decoupling object and scene rendering. It solves the problem of occlusion handling during object insertion by merging the rendered object and the pre-rendered scene using a 3D-aware opacity map. Unlike standard direct volume ray casting which renders the entire volume simultaneously, this method isolates the object's volume within a specific 3D bounding box and uses depth comparison against the static scene to determine visibility.
+A method for integrating newly generated 3D objects into an existing Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) scene by decoupling the rendering of the object from the background scene. It sets itself apart by performing the final alpha-blending in image space using a pre-computed scene cache, rather than accumulating the entire scene in a single ray-casting pass. Choose this method when occlusion-aware compositing of dynamic objects into static neural scenes is needed.
+
+### [Shading and Occlusion Models](15337-shading-and-occlusion-models.md)
+A family that extends Direct Volume Ray Casting by modifying the compositing step to incorporate lighting, shadows, and occlusion cues, thereby enhancing depth perception and visual clarity. It sets itself apart by using simplified or image-space techniques rather than full physically-based surface/volume interaction models. Choose this method when interactive volume rendering requires enhanced depth cues without the computational cost of full global illumination.
+
+### [WYSIWYP (What You See Is What You Pick)](16498-wysiwyp-what-you-see-is-what-you-pick.md)
+A volume picking technique that enables users to intuitively select 3D spatial positions in volumetric renderings based solely on the volume data, transfer function, and visibility perception. It sets itself apart by analyzing the derivative of accumulated opacity to detect visible structures for interaction, rather than integrating color and opacity for display. Choose this method when precise 3D spatial selection in interactive volume visualization is required.
+
+### [Auditory Volume Rendering](18941-auditory-volume-rendering.md)
+A rendering technique that maps scalar field values to auditory properties (pitch, amplitude, duration) using an auditory transfer function, generating sound via a ray-marching procedure similar to optical volume rendering. It sets itself apart by outputting auditory data instead of visual images. Choose this method for audio-visual data exploration or accessibility applications.
+
+### [Spherical Coordinate Ray-Casting Volume Rendering](19598-spherical-coordinate-ray-casting-volume-rendering.md)
+A ray-casting method to visualize 3D power volumetric data of EME on a globe, handling non-regular grid data organized in spherical coordinates. It sets itself apart by supporting spherical coordinate systems instead of Cartesian grids. Choose this method for geospatial or spherical volumetric data visualization.
+
+### [All-in-Focus Neural Radiance Fields](19657-all-in-focus-neural-radiance-fields.md)
+A framework to synthesize all-in-focus (AiF) novel views from dual-camera image pairs (main and ultra-wide) where the main camera has consistent defocus blur due to fixed autofocus on a prominent object. It sets itself apart by explicitly handling consistent defocus blur to produce sharp, all-in-focus novel views. Choose this method when generating sharp novel views from defocused dual-camera captures is required.
+
+### [PRT-based Volume Rendering Approximation](19776-prt-based-volume-rendering-approximation.md)
+A method to approximate in-scattering in volume rendering using Precomputed Radiance Transfer (PRT) and Spherical Harmonics (SH) to replace expensive double integrals with dot products and cube map lookups. It sets itself apart by using PRT/SH approximations instead of direct integration or path tracing. Choose this method when real-time in-scattering approximation is needed without full path tracing costs.
+
+### [Texture-Enhanced Direct Volume Rendering](20022-texture-enhanced-direct-volume-rendering.md)
+A methodology for direct volume rendering that extends the standard color-opacity transfer function process by enabling the use of textures to encode additional information (e.g., fiber direction, cellular abnormalities) throughout the volume in three dimensions. It sets itself apart by integrating volumetric textures instead of just scalar values. Choose this method when rich, multi-channel volumetric data visualization is required.
+
+### [True Volume Aurora](20150-true-volume-aurora.md)
+A volume rendering method for aurorae based on a height-based model, GPU-friendly atmosphere model, efficient ray marching with distance fields, and interactive frame rates. It sets itself apart by using a physically-based electron deposition function and spline-based footprint simulation. Choose this method for real-time, physically accurate aurora visualization.
 
 ### Two Level Volume Rendering with Layers
 A technique that achieves results similar to traditional two-level volume rendering by rendering different styles (e.g., different transfer functions or rendering modes like MIP vs DVR) into separate layers and compositing them, rather than selecting rendering types per ray segment during integration [S2].
-*   **Step:** Render each distinct material style or transfer function into a separate layer (e.g., DVR for soft tissue, DVR for bones). Composite the separate layers into a final image using a compositing function that maps the resulting layers to the view volume [S2].
